Yes, you can modify the `default.conf.template` file to include a default host in addition to the virtual host specified by the `${NGINX_HOST}` variable. To do this, you will need to add a new `server` block to the file.

Here is an example of what the modified `default.conf.template` file might look like:
```
server {
  listen 80 default_server;
  listen [::]:80 default_server;
  server_name _;
  return 444;
}

server {
  listen 80;
  listen [::]:80;
  server_name ${NGINX_HOST};
  return 301 https://${NGINX_HOST}$request_uri;
}

server {
  listen 443 ssl http2;
  listen [::]:443 ssl http2;
  server_name ${NGINX_HOST};
  root /app;

  ssl_certificate /etc/nginx/ssl/example.com.crt;
  ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/ssl/example.com.key;
  include /etc/nginx/ssl/letsencrypt/options-ssl-nginx.conf;
  ssl_dhparam /etc/nginx/ssl/letsencrypt/ssl-dhparams.pem;

  location / {
    try_files $uri $uri/ /index.php$is_args$args;
  }

  location ~ \.php$ {
    try_files $uri =404;
    fastcgi_pass php:9000;
    fastcgi_param SCRIPT_FILENAME $document_root$fastcgi_script_name;
    include fastcgi_params;
  }
}
```
In this example, the first `server` block listens on port 80 and responds with a 444 status code for all requests to any host other than the virtual host specified by the `${NGINX_HOST}` variable. The second `server` block redirects all HTTP requests for the virtual host to HTTPS. The third `server` block serves the virtual host over HTTPS.

To return a web page instead of just an error for an unknown host, you can modify the default host block in the `default.conf.template` file to include a `root` directive and a `location` block that serves a default page.

Here is an example of what the modified default host block might look like:
```
server {
  listen 80 default_server;
  listen [::]:80 default_server;
  server_name _;
  root /app/default;

  location / {
    try_files $uri /index.html =404;
  }
}
```
In this example, the `root` directive specifies the directory where the default page is located. The `location` block serves the `index.html` file in this directory for all requests to any host other than the virtual host specified by the `${NGINX_HOST}` variable. If the `index.html` file does not exist, a 404 error will be returned.

You will also need to make sure that the `/app/default` directory exists and contains the default page you want to serve. You can do this by creating the `default` directory within the `app` directory and adding an `index.html` file to it.

