An integration test is a type of test that verifies the end-to-end behavior of a system by exercising the interactions between components or modules. In contrast to a unit test, which only tests a single component or unit in isolation, an integration test verifies the integration between multiple components or modules.

In the case of a Java Spring Boot REST controller, an integration test would involve setting up an instance of the Spring Boot application, sending HTTP requests to the controller, and verifying the responses.

Here is an example of how an integration test for the `MyController` class in the previous example might look like:
```
@RunWith(SpringRunner.class)
@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
public class MyControllerIntegrationTest {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mvc;

    @MockBean
    private MyService myService;

    @Test
    public void testGetItems() throws Exception {
        // Setup mock service to return a list of items
        when(myService.getItems()).thenReturn(Arrays.asList("item1", "item2"));

        // Perform the HTTP GET request to the controller
        MvcResult result = mvc.perform(get("/items"))
                              .andExpect(status().isOk())
                              .andReturn();

        // Verify the response body
        assertEquals(Arrays.asList("item1", "item2"), result.getResponse().getContentAsString());
    }
}
```
In this example, the `MockMvc` class is used to send HTTP requests to the controller and verify the responses. The `@SpringBootTest` and `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` annotations are used to create an instance of the Spring Boot application and set up the `MockMvc` object, respectively. The `@MockBean` annotation is used to inject a mock implementation of the `MyService` class into the application context.

This is just one way to write an integration test for a Java Spring Boot REST controller. Depending on the specific requirements of your controller, the exact steps and implementation may vary.

