Yes, this decorator should work with Redis as well, assuming that you have configured Redis as your cache backend in your Django app.

To use Redis as your cache backend, you need to add the following settings to your `settings.py` file:
```python
CACHES = {
    'default': {
        'BACKEND': 'django_redis.cache.RedisCache',
        'LOCATION': 'redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0',
        'OPTIONS': {
            'CLIENT_CLASS': 'django_redis.client.DefaultClient',
        }
    }
}
```
This sets up the default cache backend to use Redis, with the Redis server running on `127.0.0.1:6379`. You may need to adjust the `LOCATION` setting to match your Redis server configuration.

Once you have configured Redis as your cache backend, you can use the same `cache_function` decorator to cache the result of any function in your Django app. The decorator will automatically use Redis as the cache backend, since it is configured as the default backend in your Django settings.
```python
from django.core.cache import cache
from functools import wraps

def cache_function(func):
    @wraps(func)
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
        key = f"{func.__name__}:{args}:{kwargs}"
        result = cache.get(key)
        if result is not None:
            return result
        result = func(*args, **kwargs)
        cache.set(key, result)
        return result
    return wrapper
```
With this decorator, the result of any function that is decorated with `@cache_function` will be cached in Redis, and subsequent calls to the same function with the same arguments will return the cached result instead of recomputing it.

