Here is a step-by-step guide on how to set up a reverse proxy using Nginx in a Docker container:

1. Create a Dockerfile: Create a Dockerfile that will be used to build the Docker image for the reverse proxy. The Dockerfile should contain the following:
```sql
FROM nginx:alpine

COPY nginx.conf /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
```
2. Create a configuration file: Create an Nginx configuration file called `nginx.conf` that will be used to configure the reverse proxy. The configuration file should contain the following:
```bash
worker_processes 1;

events { worker_connections 1024; }

http {
    server {
        listen 80;

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://rtmp-server:1935/live/stream-name;
            proxy_http_version 1.1;
            proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
            proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Server $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Nginx-Proxy true;
            proxy_redirect off;
            proxy_read_timeout 86400;
        }
    }
}
```
3. Build the Docker image: Use the following command to build the Docker image:
```lua
docker build -t nginx-reverse-proxy .
```
4. Start the Docker container: Use the following command to start the Docker container, linking it to the RTMP server container:
```perl
docker run -d --name nginx-reverse-proxy --link rtmp-server:rtmp-server -p 80:80 nginx-reverse-proxy
```
5. Test the reverse proxy: Test the reverse proxy by accessing the RTMP stream through the reverse proxy URL. The URL should be in the following format: `http://your-reverse-proxy-address/live/stream-name`

By following these steps, you should be able to set up a reverse proxy using Nginx in a Docker container. The reverse proxy will present a secure URL to the video player, while the RTMP server will receive the incoming RTMP stream on a different URL. This will help you hide the RTMP URL from viewers and prevent unauthorized access or misuse of your stream.

Here's an example of a Dockerfile that creates an RTMP server using Nginx and sets up a reverse proxy for it:
```bash
# Use an official Nginx image as the base image
FROM nginx:alpine

# Install the required packages
RUN apk add --no-cache build-base curl tar unzip

# Download and compile Nginx RTMP module
RUN curl https://github.com/arut/nginx-rtmp-module/archive/v1.2.1.tar.gz | tar xz \
    && cd nginx-rtmp-module-1.2.1 \
    && ./configure \
    && make \
    && make install

# Remove the source code to save disk space
RUN cd .. && rm -rf nginx-rtmp-module-1.2.1

# Copy the Nginx configuration file to the image
COPY nginx.conf /etc/nginx/nginx.conf

# Expose the RTMP and HTTP ports
EXPOSE 1935 80

# Start Nginx
CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]
```
This Dockerfile uses the official Nginx image and installs the required packages for building the Nginx RTMP module. It then downloads the Nginx RTMP module source code, compiles it, and installs it.

The Nginx configuration file, `nginx.conf`, will need to be provided and should include the RTMP and reverse proxy configuration. Here is an example of a basic Nginx configuration file:
```perl
worker_processes  1;
events {
    worker_connections  1024;
}

rtmp {
    server {
        listen 1935;
        chunk_size 4096;

        application live {
            live on;
            record off;
        }
    }
}

http {
    server {
        listen 80;
        server_name localhost;

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://localhost:1935;
            proxy_redirect off;
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        }
    }
}
```
This configuration sets up a single RTMP application named "live" and exposes it on port 1935. It also sets up an HTTP reverse proxy on port 80 that proxies requests to the RTMP application on port 1935.

With this Dockerfile and Nginx configuration, you can build an image that contains a complete RTMP server with a reverse proxy, all inside a single Docker container. To build the image, run the following command in the directory containing the Dockerfile:
```
