Sure! Here's an example of how the build-heap algorithm might be used to create a max heap from an array in Java:
```
public class Heap {
    public static void buildHeap(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        // Start from the last non-leaf node
        for (int i = n / 2 - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            heapify(arr, n, i);
        }
    }

    public static void heapify(int[] arr, int n, int i) {
        int largest = i;
        int left = 2 * i + 1;
        int right = 2 * i + 2;

        // Check if left child is larger than root
        if (left < n && arr[left] > arr[largest]) {
            largest = left;
        }

        // Check if right child is larger than root
        if (right < n && arr[right] > arr[largest]) {
            largest = right;
        }

        // If largest is not root
        if (largest != i) {
            int temp = arr[i];
            arr[i] = arr[largest];
            arr[largest] = temp;
            // Recursively heapify the affected sub-tree
            heapify(arr, n, largest);
        }
    }
}
```
This code includes two main functions: `buildHeap` and `heapify`. The `buildHeap` function takes an array of integers as input and calls the `heapify` function for each internal node in the array, starting with the last non-leaf node and moving towards the root. The `heapify` function takes an array, the size of the array, and the index of the root node of the subtree as input, and compares the value of the root node to the values of its children. If the root node is smaller than one of its children, the values of the root node and the larger child are swapped, and the function is recursively called on the affected subtree. This process continues until the entire array is a heap.

Note that this example is just one way of implementing the build-heap algorithm, and different variations of the algorithm may have slightly different implementations.

The algorithm for extracting the maximum element (also known as the root) from a max heap is called extract-max or heap-extract-max. The basic idea behind this algorithm is to remove the root of the heap and then re-heapify the remaining elements to maintain the heap property. Here is a general outline of the algorithm in pseudocode:

1. Check if the heap is empty. If it is, return an error.
2. Save the value of the root in a variable (the maximum value).
3. Replace the root with the last element of the heap (the bottom-rightmost leaf).
4. Decrement the size of the heap by 1.
5. Call the heapify function on the root node to re-heapify the remaining elements and maintain the heap property.

Here is an example of how this algorithm might be implemented in Java:
```
public class Heap {
    public static int extractMax(int[] arr) {
        if (arr.length < 1) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Heap is empty");
        }
        int max = arr[0];
        arr[0] = arr[arr.length - 1];
        heapify(arr, arr.length - 1, 0);
        return max;
    }

    public static void heapify(int[] arr, int n, int i) {
        //same implementation as above
    }
}
```
The time complexity of the extract-max algorithm is O(log n), where n is the size of the heap. The reason for this is that, in the worst case, the algorithm needs to "bubble down" the new root element from the top of the heap to the bottom, which requires log n comparisons. The space complexity is O(1) because the algorithm only needs a constant amount of extra memory to store the maximum value, and re-uses the original array to store the remaining elements.

