The `useEffect` hook is triggered after every render of a component. However, whether or not the effect runs depends on the dependencies that you specify as the second argument to the `useEffect` hook.

If you do not specify any dependencies, the effect will run after every render. This is equivalent to specifying an empty array (`[]`) as the second argument.

If you specify a list of dependencies, the effect will only run if any of the dependencies have changed since the last render. This is a performance optimization that avoids unnecessary re-runs of the effect.

For example, consider the following code:
```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function Example() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    console.log('Effect ran');
  }, [count]);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Click me</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```
In this example, the effect will only run if the `count` state variable has changed since the last render. When the button is clicked and the `count` state is updated, the effect will run and log "Effect ran" to the console.

If you had not specified the `count` dependency in the `useEffect` call, the effect would have run after every render, regardless of whether or not the `count` state had changed.

You are correct that if you don't specify the second argument in `useEffect`, then the effect will run after every render, regardless of whether or not any state has changed.

In other words, `useEffect` without dependencies is equivalent to using `componentDidUpdate` lifecycle method in class components.

Here is an example:
```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function Example() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [name, setName] = useState('');

  useEffect(() => {
    console.log('Effect ran');
  });

  return (
    <div>
      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Click me</button>
      <input type="text" value={name} onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)} />
    </div>
  );
}
```
In this example, the effect will run after every render, regardless of whether or not the `count` or `name` state has changed. Therefore, if you only want the effect to run when specific state variables change, you should specify those variables as dependencies in the second argument of `useEffect`.

Yes, if you provide a second argument to `useEffect`, the effect will be triggered on the initial render of the component, as well as on subsequent renders when the dependencies have changed.

For example, consider the following code:
```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function Example() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
