In Ceedling, you can create mocks using the `MOCK_FUNCTION` or `MOCK_VARIABLE` macros. These macros should be placed in the test file, before the test case that uses the mocks.

Here is an example of how to use the `MOCK_FUNCTION` macro to mock a function called `myFunction` that is used by the static function you are testing:
```
#include "my_source.c"

MOCK_FUNCTION_WITH_CALLTYPE(int, myFunction, (int arg1, int arg2), (arg1, arg2));

TEST_CASE( "myStaticFunction tests", "[myStaticFunction]" ) {
    // Set up the mock function's behavior
    MOCK_FUNCTION_EXPECT_CALL(myFunction).withParameter("arg1", 1).withParameter("arg2", 2).andReturn(3);

    // Test the static function
    int result = myStaticFunction();

    // Verify the result
    TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL(3, result);
}
```
In this example, the `MOCK_FUNCTION_WITH_CALLTYPE` macro is used to define the mock function `myFunction` with the same function signature as the original function.
The `MOCK_FUNCTION_EXPECT_CALL` macro is used to define the behavior of the mock function, in this example, it's expected to be called with arguments `arg1=1` and `arg2=2` and returns 3.

You can use this approach to mock any functions or variables that are used by the static function you are testing, and control their behavior during the test.

It is also important to note that the mocks should be reset after each test case, you can use `MOCK_RESET` macro to reset all the mocks.

Yes, the same syntax can be used to mock functions defined in header files.

In the case of header files, you will need to include the header file in your test file, just as you would in the source file that uses the function. Once the header file is included, you can use the `MOCK_FUNCTION` or `MOCK_VARIABLE` macros to define mocks for the functions or variables you want to control during the test.

Here is an example of how to use the `MOCK_FUNCTION` macro to mock a function called `myFunction` that is defined in a header file called `my_header.h`:
```
#include "my_header.h"

MOCK_FUNCTION_WITH_CALLTYPE(int, myFunction, (int arg1, int arg2), (arg1, arg2));

TEST_CASE( "myStaticFunction tests", "[myStaticFunction]" ) {
    // Set up the mock function's behavior
    MOCK_FUNCTION_EXPECT_CALL(myFunction).withParameter("arg1", 1).withParameter("arg2", 2).andReturn(3);

    // Test the static function
    int result = myStaticFunction();

    // Verify the result
    TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL(3, result);
}
```
Note that if the header file also includes other headers, you will also need to include them in your test file, otherwise you may get compile errors.

It's also important to keep in mind that if the function you want to mock is defined as `static` in the header file, it will only be available for use in the translation unit where it is defined, in this case you can't mock it directly in your test file.

