To store data in a session storage, you can use the `sessionStorage` object in JavaScript. The `sessionStorage` object stores data for the duration of a page session, meaning the data will be deleted when the browser is closed.

Here is an example of how you can store data in session storage before redirecting to the next page:
```
$.ajax({
    type: "GET",
    url: "http://localhost:8081/categoryname",
    data: {
      dvdTitle: dvdTitle,
      maxPrice: maxPrice,
      category: category
    },
    success: function(data) {
      // Store data in session storage
      sessionStorage.setItem("searchResults", JSON.stringify(data));
      // Redirect to the next page
      location.replace("searchResults.html");
    }
  });
```
And then in the searchResults.html you can use sessionStorage.getItem("searchResults") to retrieve the data.
```
$(document).ready(function () {
    var searchResults = JSON.parse(sessionStorage.getItem("searchResults"));
    // do something with the data
});
```
Please note that the data stored in session storage is available only on the same origin and will not be accessible from other sites.

You can also use `localStorage` object to store data that will persist even after the browser is closed. But the data stored in `localStorage` is accessible from other sites as well.

