```sql
CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE IF NOT EXISTS foreign_tables (
  name VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY
);

INSERT INTO foreign_tables (name)
SELECT DISTINCT foreigntable_name
FROM Files;

SET @sql = CONCAT(
  'SELECT * FROM Files f ',
  'WHERE NOT EXISTS (',
  '  SELECT 1 FROM ',
  (SELECT GROUP_CONCAT(name SEPARATOR ' t WHERE t.id = f.foreigntable_id) UNION ALL SELECT 1 FROM ')
  FROM foreign_tables,
  ' t WHERE t.id = f.foreigntable_id',
  ')'
);

PREPARE stmt FROM @sql;
EXECUTE stmt;
```
This query creates a temporary table called `foreign_tables` that contains the distinct names of all foreign tables used in the Files table. It then constructs a dynamic SQL statement that uses a subquery with `UNION ALL` to check for the existence of related foreign records in each foreign table. The `GROUP_CONCAT` function is used to concatenate the individual subqueries into a single string that is inserted into the dynamic SQL statement.

Note that this query assumes that each foreign table has an `id` column as its primary key. If your foreign tables have a different primary key column name, you should replace `id` with the actual name of the primary key column in the query. Also note that this query creates a temporary table, which may have implications for your application's memory usage and concurrency. You should test this query on your system to ensure that it works correctly and efficiently for your use case.

