Below is the refactored `zpl_memset` function using AVX-512 SIMD intrinsics for the x86-64 architecture. This implementation assumes the compiler and the target platform support AVX-512. Note that using AVX-512 intrinsics may require additional compiler flags, such as `-mavx512f` for GCC and Clang.
```c
#include <immintrin.h> // For AVX-512 intrinsics

// The zpl_memset function sets the first 'length' bytes of the block of memory pointed by 'destination'
// to the specified 'value'. The function uses AVX-512 SIMD intrinsics to optimize the memset operation
// for x86-64 CPUs.
//
// Parameters:
//   destination: pointer to the block of memory to fill
//   value: byte value to be set
//   length: number of bytes to be set
ZPL_IMPL_INLINE void *zpl_memset(void *destination, zpl_u8 value, zpl_isize length) {
    if (destination == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }

    zpl_u8 *dest_ptr = cast(zpl_u8 *) destination;

    // Handle the case when length is zero
    if (length == 0) {
        return destination;
    }

    // Fill remaining bytes if the destination is not 64-byte aligned
    while (((zpl_uintptr)dest_ptr) % 64 != 0 && length > 0) {
        *dest_ptr++ = value;
        length--;
    }

    // Use AVX-512 SIMD intrinsics for large memory blocks
    if (length >= 64) {
        __m512i value_vector = _mm512_set1_epi8(value);
        while (length >= 64) {
            _mm512_storeu_si512((__m512i *)dest_ptr, value_vector);
            dest_ptr += 64;
            length -= 64;
        }
    }

    // Fill remaining bytes using scalar operations
    while (length > 0) {
        *dest_ptr++ = value;
        length--;
    }

    return destination;
}
```
This refactored version of `zpl_memset` utilizes AVX-512 SIMD intrinsics to optimize the memory setting process on x86-64 CPUs. The code first aligns the destination pointer to a 64-byte boundary and fills the remaining bytes using scalar operations. Then, for large memory blocks, the function employs the AVX-512 intrinsic `_mm512_storeu_si512` to fill 64 bytes at a time with the specified value. Finally, any remaining bytes are filled using scalar operations.

