To add Let's Encrypt SSL certificates to your virtual hosts using Certbot, you will need to add a new service to your `docker-compose.yml` file. Here is an example of how you can set up Certbot in Docker Compose:
```
version: '3'

services:
  certbot:
    image: certbot/certbot
    volumes:
      - ./certbot/conf:/etc/letsencrypt
      - ./certbot/www:/var/www/certbot
    command: certonly --webroot --webroot-path=/var/www/certbot --email your@email.com --agree-tos --no-eff-email -d example.com -d www.example.com
  nginx:
    image: nginx
    volumes:
      - ./nginx.conf:/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf
      - ./sites:/etc/nginx/sites-enabled
      - ./certbot/conf:/etc/letsencrypt
    ports:
      - 80:80
      - 443:443
```
This `docker-compose.yml` file includes a new service called `certbot`, which uses the `certbot/certbot` image and runs the `certonly` command to request SSL certificates for the domains `example.com` and `www.example.com`. Replace `example.com` and `www.example.com` with your own domains, and update the `--email` option with your own email address.

The `certbot` service mounts two directories to the container: `./certbot/conf` and `./certbot/www`. The `./certbot/conf` directory is where Certbot will store the generated SSL certificates, and the `./certbot/www` directory is used as the webroot for the certificate validation process.

The `nginx` service is modified to mount the `./certbot/conf` directory, which contains the SSL certificates generated by Certbot. The `nginx` service is also updated to listen on both port 80 and port 443, so it can serve both HTTP and HTTPS traffic.

To request the SSL certificates, run `docker-compose run certbot`. This will start the `certbot` service and request the SSL certificates. Once the certificates have been generated, they will be stored in the `./certbot/conf` directory on your host machine.

To use the SSL certificates with your virtual hosts, you will need to update your virtual host configuration files to include the SSL certificate paths. Here is an example virtual host configuration file that includes SSL support:
```
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;
    return 301 https://$server_name$request_uri;
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name example.com;
    root /var/www/example.com;

    ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/privkey.pem;
    include /etc/letsencrypt/options-ssl-nginx.conf;
    ssl_dhparam /etc/letsencrypt/ssl-dhparams.pem;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
    }
}
```

