Tabu List is a mechanism used in Tabu Search to prevent the search from revisiting the same solution or similar solutions. It works by keeping track of the recent moves that have been made and temporarily marking them as "tabu". When generating new solutions, the search will avoid making moves that are currently tabu. This allows the search to explore a wider range of solutions and avoid getting stuck in local optima.

The Tabu List typically has a fixed size and a maximum age for each move. Moves that are older than the maximum age are removed from the Tabu List and can be revisited. The size and maximum age of the Tabu List are typically determined through experimentation and can have a significant impact on the search performance.

Here's an example of how to implement a Tabu List in Dart:
```arduino
class TabuList {
  final int maxSize;
  final Map<String, int> tabuMap;

  TabuList(this.maxSize) : tabuMap = {};

  void add(String move, int tenure) {
    if (tabuMap.length >= maxSize) {
      removeOldest();
    }
    tabuMap[move] = tenure;
  }

  bool contains(String move) {
    return tabuMap.containsKey(move);
  }

  void removeOldest() {
    int oldestTenure = tabuMap.values.reduce(min);
    List<String> oldestMoves = tabuMap.entries.where((entry) => entry.value == oldestTenure).map((entry) => entry.key).toList();
    String oldestMove = oldestMoves[0]; // Choose a random move among the oldest moves
    tabuMap.remove(oldestMove);
  }

  void decrementTenure() {
    List<String> movesToRemove = [];
    tabuMap.forEach((move, tenure) {
      if (tenure <= 1) {
        movesToRemove.add(move);
      } else {
        tabuMap[move] = tenure - 1;
      }
    });
    movesToRemove.forEach((move) => tabuMap.remove(move));
  }
}
```
In this example, the `TabuList` class represents the Tabu List and has a maximum size (`maxSize`) and a `tabuMap` that maps each move to its tenure (i.e., how long it should be marked as tabu). The `add` method is used to add a move to the Tabu List, the `contains` method is used to check if a move is tabu, the `removeOldest` method is used to remove the oldest move from the Tabu List, and the `decrementTenure` method is used to decrease the tenure of each move in the Tabu List. The `removeOldest` method chooses a random move among the oldest moves to remove, to avoid biasing the search towards a particular move.

Aspiration criteria is a mechanism used in tabu search to allow some moves that violate the tabu list if they lead to a better solution. It is used to avoid getting stuck in a local optima. When a move that is in the tabu list is selected and it leads to a better solution than the current best solution, the aspiration criteria is used to allow that move to be accepted.

The aspiration criteria checks if the move violates the tabu list, but still improves the current best solution. If the move meets both conditions, it is accepted and added to the tabu list as a non-tabu move.

Here is an example of how to implement aspiration criteria in dart:
```scss
// Evaluate the fitness of the current solution
double currentFitness = evaluateFitness(currentSolution);

// Generate a set of neighboring solutions
List<Solution> neighbors = generateNeighbors(currentSolution);

// Find the best neighbor that is not in the tabu list or satisfies aspiration criteria
Solution bestNeighbor = null;
for (Solution neighbor in neighbors) {
    // Check if the neighbor violates the tabu list
    if (isTabuMove(neighbor)) {
        continue;
    }

    // Evaluate the fitness of the neighbor
    double neighborFitness = evaluateFitness(neighbor);

    // Check if the neighbor is better than the current best solution
    if (neighborFitness > currentFitness) {
        // Allow the move if it satisfies aspiration criteria
        if (satisfiesAspirationCriteria(neighbor)) {
            bestNeighbor = neighbor;
            break;
        }
    }

    // Update the best neighbor if the current neighbor is better
    if (bestNeighbor == null || neighborFitness > evaluateFitness(bestNeighbor)) {
        bestNeighbor = neighbor;
    }
}

// Update the tabu list
updateTabuList(bestNeighbor);

// Update the current solution
currentSolution = bestNeighbor;
```
In the above code, `isTabuMove` is a function that checks if a move is in the tabu list, and `satisfiesAspirationCriteria` is a function that checks if a move satisfies the aspiration criteria. The `updateTabuList` function is used to add the selected move to the tabu list.

Acceptance criteria in Tabu search refers to the rules that determine whether to accept or reject a new solution generated by the Neighborhood Search. The acceptance criterion is usually based on the fitness improvement of the new solution or the probability of accepting a worse solution.

One common acceptance criterion is based on the Metropolis-Hastings algorithm, which is used in simulated annealing. According to this criterion, a new solution is always accepted if it improves the fitness of the current solution. If the new solution is worse than the current solution, it is still accepted with a certain probability, which is calculated using the following formula:

p = exp((f\_new - f\_current) / T)

where p is the probability of accepting the new solution, f\_new is the fitness of the new solution, f\_current is the fitness of the current solution, and T is the current temperature of the system.

As the temperature decreases, the probability of accepting a worse solution decreases, and the algorithm becomes more greedy. In the end, the algorithm converges to a local optimum.

Here is an example implementation of the acceptance criterion based on the Metropolis-Hastings algorithm in Dart:
```scss
double temperature = initialTemperature;
double coolingRate = 0.01;

while (temperature > 1.0) {
  // Generate a new solution using Neighborhood Search
  Solution newSolution = generateNeighbor(currentSolution);

  // Calculate the fitness of the new solution
  double newFitness = evaluateFitness(newSolution);

  // Calculate the energy difference between the current and new solutions
  double energyDifference = newFitness - currentFitness;

  // Accept the new solution if it improves the fitness
  if (energyDifference < 0) {
    currentSolution = newSolution;
    currentFitness = newFitness;
  } else {
    // Accept the new solution with a certain probability
    double acceptanceProbability = exp(-energyDifference / temperature);
    if (acceptanceProbability > Math.Random().nextDouble()) {
      currentSolution = newSolution;
      currentFitness = newFitness;
    }
  }

  // Update the temperature
  temperature *= 1 - coolingRate;
}
```

