Here's an example of how to implement load balancing using NGINX:

1. Install NGINX: You can install NGINX on your server by running the following command:
```csharp
sudo apt-get install nginx
```
2. Configure NGINX: You can configure NGINX to act as a load balancer by editing the `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf` file. Here's an example configuration file:
```c
http {
    upstream backend {
        server mern-stack-web-app-1:3000;
        server mern-stack-web-app-2:3000;
        server mern-stack-web-app-3:3000;
    }

    server {
        listen 80;

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://backend;
        }
    }
}
```
In this configuration file, we define an upstream group called `backend` that includes three instances of our MERN stack web application running on ports 3000, and then define a server that listens on port 80 and proxies requests to the `backend` group.

3. Restart NGINX: After you've made changes to the NGINX configuration file, you'll need to restart the NGINX service to apply the changes. You can do this by running the following command:
```
sudo systemctl restart nginx
```
That's it! Now, NGINX will distribute incoming requests across the three instances of your MERN stack web application running on ports 3000, providing load balancing and improving your application's scalability.

