The issue you're facing is likely due to how your server or development environment is set up. When you directly enter the URL in the address bar, your server must return the correct HTML file (usually `index.html`) that contains the React app. If it does not, the app will not boot up, and you will not be able to navigate to the correct route.

Since you mentioned that you're working on your local without a server, I'll assume you're using a development server provided by a tool like `create-react-app`. By default, `create-react-app` should already be configured to always return the `index.html` file when you directly enter the URL in the address bar. If you've ejected or made changes to your configuration, you might need to check your development server setup to ensure it handles this correctly.

If you're using a custom server, you'll need to ensure the server is configured to return the `index.html` file for all routes that are not static assets. Here's an example of how you can do this using Express.js:
```javascript
const express = require("express");
const path = require("path");

const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

app.use(express.static(path.join(__dirname, "build")));

app.get("/*", (req, res) => {
  res.sendFile(path.join(__dirname, "build", "index.html"));
});

app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server is running on port ${PORT}`);
});
```
This configuration serves static assets from the `build` directory and returns the `index.html` file for all other requests. This way, when you enter a URL directly into the address bar, the server will return the `index.html` file, allowing your React app to boot up and navigate to the correct route.

