In a Spring MVC web application, you can bind a form to a model by mapping the form input fields to the properties of a model object. This process is called data binding, and it allows you to easily populate a model object with data from a form submission.

To bind a form to a model in Spring MVC, you typically define a Java class that represents the model object, with properties that correspond to the form input fields. For example, consider the following HTML form:
```html
<form action="/submit" method="POST">
  <input type="text" name="username" value="">
  <input type="text" name="email" value="">
  <input type="submit" value="Submit">
</form>
```
You can define a model class that corresponds to this form as follows:
```java
public class User {
  private String username;
  private String email;

  // getters and setters
  // ...
}
```
In your Spring MVC controller method, you can then use the `@ModelAttribute` annotation to bind the form data to a `User` object:
```java
@PostMapping("/submit")
public String handleSubmit(@ModelAttribute("user") User user) {
  // Process the user object
  // ...
  return "result";
}
```
In this example, the `@ModelAttribute("user")` annotation binds the form data to a `User` object and assigns it the name `"user"` in the model. The `User` object can then be used to process the form data in the controller method.

Data binding is a powerful feature of Spring MVC that can save you a lot of time and effort when processing form submissions. It allows you to easily map form data to model objects and simplifies the process of handling user input in your web application.

Object binding is the process of mapping HTTP request parameters to model objects in a web application, typically in a Model-View-Controller (MVC) framework like Spring MVC. It is a form of data binding that allows you to automatically populate model objects with data from HTTP requests, rather than manually extracting the data from the request parameters and setting it on the model objects.

In Spring MVC, object binding is typically performed using the `@ModelAttribute` annotation. When this annotation is used on a method parameter, Spring MVC will attempt to automatically populate the parameter with data from the HTTP request parameters. For example, consider the following method in a Spring MVC controller:
```java
@PostMapping("/submit")
public String handleSubmit(@ModelAttribute("user") User user) {
  // Process the user object
  // ...
  return "result";
}
```
In this example, the `@ModelAttribute("user")` annotation tells Spring MVC to bind the HTTP request parameters to a `User` object and add it to the model with the name "user". Spring MVC will automatically map the request parameters to the properties of the `User` object based on their names.

Object binding in Spring MVC can also handle more complex types, such as nested objects and collections. For example, if a form contains multiple fields with the same name, Spring MVC can automatically populate a list or array of objects based on the values of those fields.

Object binding is a powerful feature of Spring MVC that simplifies the process of handling user input in web applications. It allows you to automatically populate model objects with data from HTTP requests, saving you time and effort in your web development projects.

In a Spring Boot application with an embedded Tomcat server, the `WebApplicationContext` is a specialized application context that is used to configure and manage the web components of the application. It is responsible for managing the lifecycle of the web application, handling HTTP requests and responses, and providing access to web-related services such as session management and URL mapping.

The `WebApplicationContext` is created automatically when you start your Spring Boot application with the embedded Tomcat server. It is typically configured in the `Application.java` file using the `@SpringBootApplication` annotation, which enables the web components of the application.

Here is an example of how to configure the `WebApplicationContext` in a Spring Boot application with embedded Tomcat:
```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class Application {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
  }

  @Bean
  public ServletWebServerFactory servletContainer() {
    TomcatServletWebServerFactory factory = new TomcatServletWebServerFactory();
    factory.setPort(8080);
    return factory;
  }

  @Bean
  public WebMvcConfigurer corsConfigurer() {
    return new WebMvcConfigurer() {
      @Override
      public void addCorsMappings(CorsRegistry registry) {
        registry.addMapping("/**").allowedOrigins("*");
      }
    };
  }
}
```
In this example, the `@SpringBootApplication` annotation configures the `WebApplicationContext` automatically, and the `servletContainer()` and `corsConfigurer()` methods are used to customize the configuration of the embedded Tomcat server and add a CORS filter to the application.

Overall, the `WebApplicationContext` is a crucial component of Spring Boot applications with embedded Tomcat servers, and provides a powerful and flexible way to configure and manage web components in a Spring-based environment.

