Heap sort is a sorting algorithm that works by first building a heap data structure from the elements to be sorted, and then repeatedly extracting the maximum element from the heap and placing it at the end of the sorted array. The heap is then reconstructed from the remaining elements, and the process is repeated until all elements have been sorted.

Here is an example of the heap sort algorithm in C++:
```css
void heapify(int arr[], int n, int i) {
    int largest = i;
    int l = 2*i + 1;
    int r = 2*i + 2;

    if (l < n && arr[l] > arr[largest])
        largest = l;

    if (r < n && arr[r] > arr[largest])
        largest = r;

    if (largest != i) {
        swap(arr[i], arr[largest]);
        heapify(arr, n, largest);
    }
}

void heapSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = n / 2 - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        heapify(arr, n, i);

    for (int i=n-1; i>=0; i--) {
        swap(arr[0], arr[i]);
        heapify(arr, i, 0);
    }
}
```
Use cases:
Heap sort is a good choice for sorting large datasets, particularly if you are working with data that does not fit into memory. It is also useful when you need to keep the data partially sorted during the sorting process.

Best case: O(nlogn)
Worst case: O(nlogn)

Important points for interviews:

* Heap sort is an in-place algorithm, meaning it does not require additional memory beyond the array being sorted.
* Heap sort is not stable, meaning that it may change the relative order of equal elements in the input array.
* While heap sort has the same worst-case time complexity as quicksort and mergesort, it can be slower in practice due to its relatively high overhead in building and maintaining the heap data structure.

Quicksort and Mergesort are two popular sorting algorithms used in computer science.

Quicksort:
Quicksort is an efficient in-place sorting algorithm that works by selecting a pivot element from the array and partitioning the other elements into two sub-arrays, according to whether they are less than or greater than the pivot. The sub-arrays are then recursively sorted. The pivot selection and partitioning process continues until the array is sorted.

Example in C++:
```scss
void quicksort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    if (low < high) {
        int pivot = partition(arr, low, high);
        quicksort(arr, low, pivot - 1);
        quicksort(arr, pivot + 1, high);
    }
}

int partition(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    int pivot = arr[high];
    int i = low - 1;
    for (int j = low; j <= high - 1; j++) {
        if (arr[j] < pivot) {
            i++;
            swap(arr[i], arr[j]);
        }
    }
    swap(arr[i + 1], arr[high]);
    return i + 1;
}
```
Use Cases:
Quicksort is commonly used for sorting large data sets and is often used in applications where the sorting needs to be performed quickly. It is also used in some database management systems and programming languages.

Best Case:
O(n log n)

Worst Case:
O(n^2)

Important Points:

* Quicksort is an in-place sorting algorithm, which means it does not require any additional memory.
* The choice of pivot element can greatly affect the efficiency of the algorithm.
* Quicksort is not a stable sorting algorithm, meaning that it may change the relative order of equal elements.

Mergesort:
Mergesort is a divide-and-conquer algorithm that works by dividing an array into two halves, recursively sorting the two halves, and then merging the sorted halves. It is an efficient, general-purpose, comparison-based sorting algorithm.

Example in C++:
```scss
void mergesort(int arr[], int l, int r) {
    if (l < r) {
        int m = (l + r) / 2;
        mergesort(arr, l, m);
        mergesort(arr, m + 1, r);
        merge(arr, l, m, r);
    }
}

void merge(int arr[], int l, int m, int r) {
    int n1 = m - l + 1;
    int n2 = r - m;

    int L[n1], R[n2];

    for (int i = 0; i < n1; i++) {
        L[i] = arr[l + i];
    }
    for (int j = 0; j < n2; j++) {
        R[j] = arr[m + 1 + j];
    }

    int i = 0;
    int j = 0;
    int k = l;

    while (i < n1 && j < n2) {
        if (L[i] <= R[j]) {
            arr[k] = L[i];
            i++;
        }
        else {
            arr[k] = R[j];
            j++;
        }
        k++;
    }

    while (i < n1) {
        arr[k] = L[i];
        i++;
        k++;
    }

    while (j < n2) {
        arr[k] = R[j];
        j++;
        k++;
    }
}
```
