The Liskov substitution principle (LSP) is a fundamental principle in object-oriented programming that states that subtypes must be substitutable for their base types without affecting the correctness of the program. In other words, any instance of a base class should be able to be replaced by an instance of a derived class without changing the behavior of the program.

Here's an example in Java to illustrate the Liskov substitution principle:
```java
public class Shape {
    public int getArea() {
        return 0;
    }
}

public class Rectangle extends Shape {
    private int width;
    private int height;

    public Rectangle(int width, int height) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }

    public int getWidth() {
        return width;
    }

    public int getHeight() {
        return height;
    }

    public int getArea() {
        return width * height;
    }
}

public class Square extends Shape {
    private int side;

    public Square(int side) {
        this.side = side;
    }

    public int getSide() {
        return side;
    }

    public int getArea() {
        return side * side;
    }
}
```
In this example, we have a base class called `Shape` that has a method called `getArea()` that returns the area of the shape. We also have two derived classes, `Rectangle` and `Square`, which represent different types of shapes.

The `Rectangle` class has `width` and `height` fields, and it overrides the `getArea()` method to return the area of the rectangle. The `Square` class has a `side` field, and it also overrides the `getArea()` method to return the area of the square.

Both the `Rectangle` and `Square` classes are substitutable for the `Shape` class, as they inherit the `getArea()` method from the `Shape` class and implement it in their own way. This means that we can use instances of `Rectangle` and `Square` wherever a `Shape` object is expected, without affecting the correctness of the program.

For example, consider the following code:
```java
public void printArea(Shape shape) {
    System.out.println("Area: " + shape.getArea());
}

Rectangle rectangle = new Rectangle(5, 10);
Square square = new Square(5);

printArea(rectangle); // prints "Area: 50"
printArea(square);   // prints "Area: 25"
```
In this code, we have a method called `printArea()` that takes a `Shape` object as a parameter and prints its area. We then create instances of `Rectangle` and `Square` and pass them to the `printArea()` method. Since both `Rectangle` and `Square` are substitutable for `Shape`, the `printArea()` method works correctly for both types of objects.

By adhering to the Liskov substitution principle, we can create more flexible and reusable code that is easier to maintain and extend over time.

The Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) is a principle in object-oriented programming that states that clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use. In other words, interfaces should be designed to be as specific and narrow as possible to avoid forcing clients to implement unnecessary methods.

Here's an example in Java to illustrate the Interface Segregation Principle:
```java
public interface Animal {
    void eat();
    void sleep();
    void fly();
}

public class Bird implements Animal {
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Bird is eating.");
    }

    public void sleep() {
        System.out.println("Bird is sleeping.");
    }

    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Bird is flying.");
    }
}

public class Dog implements Animal {
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Dog is eating.");
    }

    public void sleep() {
        System.out.println("Dog is sleeping.");
    }

    public void fly() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Dog cannot fly.");
    }
}
```
In this example, we have an `Animal` interface that has three methods: `eat()`, `sleep()`, and `fly()`. We also have two classes, `Bird` and `Dog`, that implement the `Animal` interface.

The `Bird` class implements all three methods of the `Animal` interface, as birds can eat, sleep, and fly. However, the `Dog` class only implements `eat()` and `sleep()`, as dogs cannot fly. Instead of implementing the `fly()` method, the `Dog` class throws an `UnsupportedOperationException`.

By following the Interface Segregation Principle, we avoid forcing the `Dog` class to implement a method that it does not need or use. This makes our code more maintainable and extensible, as we can easily add new types of animals that have different capabilities without affecting the existing classes.

For example, consider the following code:
```java
public void doSomething(Animal animal) {
    animal.eat();
    animal.sleep();
    animal.fly();
}

Bird bird = new Bird();
Dog dog = new Dog();

doSomething(bird); // prints "Bird is eating.", "Bird is sleeping.", "Bird is flying."
doSomething(dog);  // prints "Dog is eating.", "Dog is sleeping.", throws an exception
```
In this code, we have a method called `doSomething()` that takes an `Animal` object as a parameter and calls its `eat()`, `sleep()`, and `fly()` methods. We then create instances of `Bird` and `Dog` and pass them to the `doSomething()` method. Since the `Dog` class does not implement the `fly()` method, an exception is thrown when we try to call it.

By adhering to the Interface Segregation Principle, we can create more flexible and reusable code that is easier to maintain and extend over time.

